{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["<b>Then Moses set apart three cities.</b> In connecting this verse to the preceding matter, the commentators went out to gather explanations and found no sufficient clarification — for what is the force of the word 'then'? And now incline your ear and hear, for we find that a person begins a commandment even though it cannot come to completion through him — any commandment that comes to hand, one is obligated to fulfill it (Makkot 10a) — as we find with David, who said to Solomon his son: 'וְהִנֵּה בְעָנְיִי הֲכִינוֹתִי לְבֵית ה׳ זָהָב' — 'And behold, in my affliction I have prepared for the house of HaShem gold' (I Chronicles 22:14), even though he knew that he could not complete the commandment, for he was not permitted to build the Temple; nevertheless, he began the commandment even though he knew with certainty that it would not reach its fulfillment and its good except through his son. By way of parable: an old man who plants a tree for an etrog of a commandment, even though he knows with certainty that he will not be able to come to fulfill this commandment — nevertheless his sons can come to fulfill it. And many similar matters are found along this pattern. And upon this it is said in the preceding verse: 'וְשָׁמַרְתָּ אֶת חֻקָּיו וְאֶת מִצְוֺתָיו אֲשֶׁר אָנֹכִי מְצַוְּךָ הַיּוֹם אֲשֶׁר יִיטַב לְךָ וּלְבָנֶיךָ אַחֲרֶיךָ' — that is to say: if some commandment comes to your hand, fulfill it today immediately, even though its good is not completed and seen immediately but only in the time when your children after you shall complete the commandment — nevertheless you shall do the commandment today immediately, whether 'it shall be good for you,' meaning that it is within your power to complete it, or whether 'it shall be good for your children after you,' for it is not within your power to complete it. Therefore it says 'לְבָנֶיךָ אַחֲרֶיךָ' — 'your children after you' — and the word 'אַחֲרֶיךָ' ('after you') seems superfluous, yet according to our words it fits well. And upon this it is said: 'Then Moses set apart three cities' — even though they would not give refuge until those three in the land of Canaan were set apart as well, nevertheless Moses said: a commandment that has come to my hand, I shall fulfill. And this is the force of 'then' — when he taught Israel this matter in the preceding verse, he immediately acted upon it in practice in a like case, so that from him they would see and do likewise in all the Torah and its commandments. And upon this it is said: 'וְזֹאת הַתּוֹרָה אֲשֶׁר שָׂם מֹשֶׁה לִפְנֵי בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל' — that they should act in like manner in all the commandments of the Torah. And he further alluded to the saying of our Sages of blessed memory (Avot 4:18): 'Be exiled to a place of Torah,' for just as the cities of refuge give refuge and one flees to these cities and lives — so too in the Torah and its commandments it is said (Leviticus 18:5): 'וָחַי בָּהֶם' — 'and you shall live by them.'" "These Ten Commandments differ in several places from those in the portion of Yitro, and the explanation for all of them you will find above, in the portion of Yitro."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:33.576879382Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}